<body>

<header class="mast">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow"><span class="bar"></span>A chronology · Spring 1989</div>
    <h1 class="title">Tiananmen Square, <em>April&nbsp;–&nbsp;June 1989</em></h1>
    <p class="sub">Seven weeks when students, workers, and ordinary citizens occupied the symbolic heart of Beijing — and the moment the state answered them with the army.</p>
    <div class="meta-row">
      <span>Location<b>Beijing, China</b></span>
      <span>Duration<b>15 April – 9 June 1989</b></span>
      <span>Outcome<b>Military suppression</b></span>
      <span>Death toll<b>Disputed — see below</b></span>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="intro wrap">
  <div class="label">Context</div>
  <div>
    <p>The protests that converged on Tiananmen Square grew from the mourning of Hu Yaobang, a reformist former Communist Party leader who died on 15 April 1989. Within days, what began as a vigil became a demand — for freedom of the press, accountability for officials, and an end to corruption within the Party.</p>
    <p>At its height the movement drew over a million people into the square. The leadership in Zhongnanhai fractured. On the night of 3–4 June, the People's Liberation Army entered central Beijing with live ammunition. The killing has never been officially reckoned with in China, where public discussion remains censored to this day.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="timeline wrap">
  <div class="tl-head">
    <h2>The Chronology</h2>
    <span class="count">Fifteen entries</span>
  </div>

  <div class="entries">

    <article class="entry">
      <div class="date reveal"><span class="day">15</span><span class="mon">April</span></div>
      <div class="dot"></div>
      <div class="body reveal">
        <h3>Hu Yaobang dies</h3>
        <p>The former General Secretary of the Chinese Communist Party, ousted in 1987 for his liberal sympathies, suffers a heart attack during a politburo meeting and dies at 73. By evening, students are laying wreaths at the Monument to the People's Heroes in Tiananmen Square.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="entry">
      <div class="date reveal"><span class="day">22</span><span class="mon">April</span></div>
      <div class="dot"></div>
      <div class="body reveal">
        <h3>The memorial service</h3>
        <p>Tens of thousands gather outside the Great Hall of the People during Hu's official farewell. Three students kneel on the steps holding a petition, asking Premier Li Peng to receive them. He does not come out. The refusal hardens the movement's resolve.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="entry">
      <div class="date reveal"><span class="day">26</span><span class="mon">April</span></div>
      <div class="dot"></div>
      <div class="body reveal">
        <h3>The People's Daily editorial</h3>
        <p>The Party's flagship newspaper condemns the protests as "turmoil" engineered by a "tiny handful of conspirators." The language echoes the editorials that preceded the Cultural Revolution. Rather than dispersing the crowds, the editorial is read as a declaration of war — the next day, 50,000 students march through Beijing.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="entry">
      <div class="date reveal"><span class="day">04</span><span class="mon">May</span></div>
      <div class="dot"></div>
      <div class="body reveal">
        <h3>The march on the seventieth anniversary</h3>
        <p>On the anniversary of the 1919 May Fourth Movement, an estimated 100,000 students march from university districts into the square, demanding dialogue and democratic reform. The demonstrations spread to more than 400 cities across China.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="entry feature">
      <div class="date reveal"><span class="day">13</span><span class="mon">May</span></div>
      <div class="dot"></div>
      <div class="body reveal">
        <h3>The hunger strike begins</h3>
        <p>Several hundred students sit down in the square and refuse to eat, four days before Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev is due in Beijing for a historic summit — a visit meant to signal Sino-Soviet reconciliation. The world's press, in town for Gorbachev, turns its cameras on the square. Within days the hunger strikers number over 3,000; many are hospitalised.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="entry">
      <div class="date reveal"><span class="day">18</span><span class="mon">May</span></div>
      <div class="dot"></div>
      <div class="body reveal">
        <h3>Li Peng meets the students</h3>
        <p>Behind closed doors, Premier Li Peng holds a televised meeting with student leaders including Wuer Kaixi and Wang Dan. The exchange is confrontational and yields nothing. The hardline faction, supported by Deng Xiaoping, is by now determined to clear the square.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="entry">
      <div class="date reveal"><span class="day">19</span><span class="mon">May</span></div>
      <div class="dot"></div>
      <div class="body reveal">
        <h3>Zhao Ziyang's last public appearance</h3>
        <p>General Secretary Zhao Ziyang — who has argued for dialogue — arrives in the square at dawn and addresses the hunger striakers. "We have come too late," he tells them. He would not appear in public again. He spent the next fifteen years under house arrest until his death in 2005.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="entry feature">
      <div class="date reveal"><span class="day">20</span><span class="mon">May</span></div>
      <div class="dot"></div>
      <div class="body reveal">
        <h3>Martial law declared</h3>
        <p>Li Peng announces martial law on state television. Troops advance toward the city — and are stopped. Tens of thousands of ordinary Beijingers pour onto the avenues, surrounding the convoys with their bodies, offering soldiers food and water, and arguing with them through the night. For two days the people hold the capital.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="entry">
      <div class="date reveal"><span class="day">23</span><span class="mon">May</span></div>
      <div class="dot"></div>
      <div class="body reveal">
        <h3>The defaced portrait</h3>
        <p>Three men from Hunan — Yu Dongyue, Lu Decheng, and Yu Zhijian — throw ink-filled eggs at the giant portrait of Mao Ziyang on Tiananmen Gate. They are beaten by students, who fear the act will be used to discredit the movement. All three would serve long prison sentences.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="entry">
      <div class="date reveal"><span class="day">30</span><span class="mon">May</span></div>
      <div class="dot"></div>
      <div class="body reveal">
        <h3>The Goddess of Democracy</h3>
        <p>Students from the Central Academy of Fine Arts unveil a ten-metre foam-and-plaster statue facing the portrait of Mao. Modelled partly on the Statue of Liberty, it is erected overnight. The crowd names her the Goddess of Democracy.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="entry">
      <div class="date reveal"><span class="day">02</span><span class="mon">June</span></div>
      <div class="dot"></div>
      <div class="body reveal">
        <h3>The order is given</h3>
        <p>Deng Xiaoping and the Party elders authorise the use of force to clear the square by dawn on 4 June. Army units receive orders to advance on the centre of Beijing from multiple directions.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="entry feature">
      <div class="date reveal"><span class="day">03–04</span><span class="mon">June</span></div>
      <div class="dot"></div>
      <div class="body reveal">
        <h3>The massacre</h3>
        <p>From late evening on 3 June, units of the People's Liberation Army — including the 38th Army Corps, supported by tanks and armoured vehicles — open fire on civilians at intersections across western Beijing, most notably at Muxidi. By dawn, the army reaches Tiananmen Square. The last students leave the square under negotiation; the Goddess of Democracy is torn down. Killings continue through the day along the Avenue of Eternal Peace.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="entry">
      <div class="date reveal"><span class="day">05</span><span class="mon">June</span></div>
      <div class="dot"></div>
      <div class="body reveal">
        <h3>Tank Man</h3>
        <p>On the morning after, a lone man carrying shopping bags steps in front of a column of tanks leaving the square. He shifts position to block the lead tank as it tries to pass. Four photographs by foreign press photographers make him one of the most recognisable figures of the twentieth century. His identity and fate remain unknown.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="entry">
      <div class="date reveal"><span class="day">06</span><span class="mon">June</span></div>
      <div class="dot"></div>
      <div class="body reveal">
        <h3>The official account</h3>
        <p>State television announces that "a rebellion has been quelled." The

Here's a single-file HTML page presenting a timeline of the 1989 Tiananmen Square protests and the June 4th massacre, with a somber editorial design suited to the gravity of the subject.

