    color:var(--muted);
    display:flex;justify-content:space-between;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:14px;
  }
  footer a{color:var(--ink);text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1px solid var(--accent)}
  footer a:hover{background:var(--accent);color:var(--paper)}

  @media (max-width:600px){
    header.masthead{grid-template-columns:1fr}
    .masthead .meta{text-align:left}
    .timeline{margin-left:6px}
    .entry{padding-left:30px}
    .entry::before{left:-6px}
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){
    .entry{opacity:1;transform:none;transition:none}
  }
</style>
</head>
<body class="grain">

<header class="masthead">
  <div>
    <div class="kicker">China · Spring 1989 · A Chronicle</div>
    <h1>Tiananmen Square<span class="sub">April 15 – June 5, 1989</span></h1>
  </div>
  <div class="meta">
    <strong>Beijing, PRC</strong>
    39°54′N · 116°23′E<br>
    Duration: 52 days
  </div>
</header>

<main>

  <p class="lede">
    What began as a student mourning for a reformist official became, over seven weeks, the largest popular
    demonstration in the history of the People's Republic — and ended with the People's Liberation Army firing
    on its own citizens in the streets around the square. <em>No officially recognized death toll has ever
    been published. The figures below reflect the range of independent estimates.</em>
  </p>

  <div class="timeline">

    <article class="entry">
      <div class="date"><span class="daynum">15</span>April 1989</div>
      <h2>Hu Yaobang dies</h2>
      <p>Former Communist Party General Secretary Hu Yaobang, a reformist purged in 1987 for being too lenient toward student protests, suffers a heart attack and dies at 73. Within hours, students gather at Tiananmen Square to mourn — and to attach political demands to his memory.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="entry">
      <div class="date"><span class="daynum">22</span>April 1989</div>
      <h2>Mourning at the Monument</h2>
      <p>Tens of thousands of students occupy Tiananmen Square during Hu's state memorial. Outside the Great Hall of the People, three students kneel on the steps holding a petition for dialogue with Premier Li Peng. Refused an audience, the movement hardens.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="entry critical">
      <div class="date"><span class="daynum">26</span>April 1989</div>
      <h2>The People's Daily editorial</h2>
      <p>Drafted under Deng Xiaoping's authority, a front-page editorial in the Party newspaper brands the protests a "planned conspiracy" and "turmoil" — language historically associated with the Cultural Revolution's worst excesses. Rather than dispersing the students, the editorial inflames them.</p>
      <div class="pull">"A very small number of conspirators... are using the students to foment turmoil."</div>
    </article>

    <article class="entry">
      <div class="date"><span class="daynum">04</span>May 1989</div>
      <h2>One hundred thousand march</h2>
      <p>Marking the 70th anniversary of the May Fourth Movement, more than 100,000 students march through Beijing demanding press freedom, dialogue, and an end to corruption. For the first time, journalists from the official Xinhua agency join the demonstration.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="entry critical">
      <div class="date"><span class="daynum">13</span>May 1989</div>
      <h2>Hunger strike begins</h2>
      <p>Days before Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev's state visit — the first Sino-Soviet summit in 30 years — roughly 3,000 students launch a hunger strike in the square. The timing is deliberate: the world's press, already in Beijing for the summit, turns its cameras on the protesters.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="entry">
      <div class="date"><span class="daynum">15–18</span>May 1989</div>
      <h2>The world is watching</h2>
      <p>Gorbachev arrives to a capital paralyzed by over a million demonstrators. The welcoming ceremony is moved from the square to the airport. Across China, solidarity demonstrations erupt in Shanghai, Chengdu, Xi'an, and dozens of other cities.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="entry">
      <div class="date"><span class="daynum">19</span>May 1989</div>
      <h2>Zhao Ziyang's last public appearance</h2>
      <p>General Secretary Zhao Ziyang — who favored negotiation — visits hunger strikers at dawn and tells them, with tears in his eyes: "We have come too late." It is his final public appearance. He would be purged and held under house arrest for the remaining 16 years of his life.</p>
      <div class="pull">"We have come too late. We are sorry. You are still young... you must take care of yourselves."</div>
    </article>

    <article class="entry critical">
      <div class="date"><span class="daynum">20</span>May 1989</div>
      <h2>Martial law declared</h2>
      <p>The State Council declares martial law in parts of Beijing. Initial army units trying to enter the city are stopped, surrounded, and turned back by enormous crowds of unarmed civilians blocking intersections with trucks, bicycles, and their own bodies.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="entry">
      <div class="date"><span class="daynum">23–31</span>May 1989</div>
      <h2>The standoff</h2>
      <p>A week-long impaze. Troops withdraw to suburban barracks while the leadership in Zhongnanhai decides on force. Deng Xiaoping and hardliners around Li Peng prevail over the reformist wing. Estimates of those in the square range from 100,000 to over a million at various points.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="entry critical">
      <div class="date"><span class="daynum">03</span>June 1989</div>
      <h2>The army moves</h2>
      <p>Evening: tens of thousands of PLA troops, backed by tanks and armored personnel carriers, converge on central Beijing from multiple directions under orders to clear the square by dawn. Citizens again attempt to block the convoys — this time they are met with live ammunition. Civilians are killed at intersections along Chang'an Avenue and at Muxidi, Liubukou, Xidan, and Nanchizi.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="entry critical">
      <div class="date"><span class="daynum">04</span>June 1989</div>
      <h2>The massacre</h2>
      <p>In the early hours, troops and tanks clear Tiananmen Square itself and the surrounding avenues. By dawn the square is empty of protesters. Killings continue through the day and into June 5 as soldiers fire on civilians in nearby streets. Independent observers report armored vehicles crushing makeshift barricades and, in several cases, the wounded.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="entry">
      <div class="date"><span class="daynum">05</span>June 1989</div>
      <h2>Tank Man</h2>
      <p>Mid-morning, a lone man carrying shopping bags steps in front of a column of tanks leaving the square on Chang'an Avenue. He blocks their path, shifts to keep blocking them as the lead tank tries to steer around him, then climbs onto the tank to speak with its crew before being pulled away by onlookers. His identity and fate remain unknown.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="entry">
      <div class="date"><span class="daynum">06</span>June 1989</div>
      <h2>The official version</h2>
      <p>Government spokesman Yuan Mu appears on state television asserting that "not one person" was killed in the square itself and that the broader toll — including soldiers — was around 200 to 300. This figure has never been independently substantiated and remains the official line in mainland Chinese discourse on the event.</p>
    </article>

  </div>

  <section class="casualties">
    <h3>Estimated Death Toll</h3>
    <h4>No figure has ever been officially reconciled.</h4>

    <div class="toll-grid">

      <div class="toll">
        <div class="num"><span class="range">200–300</span></div>
        <div class="src">
          <strong>Chinese Government</strong>
          Stated June 6, 1989 and never revised. Includes soldiers killed by protesters.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="toll">
        <div class="num"><span class="range">2,600</span></div>
        <div class="src">
          <strong>Chinese Red Cross (initial)</strong>
          Released during the crackdown; later retracted under government pressure.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="toll">
        <div class="num"><span class="range">hundreds to thousands</span></div>
        <div class="src">
          <strong>Foreign correspondents in Beijing</strong>
          Eyewitness reports filed by BBC, Reuters, AP, and others on June 4–6.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="toll">
        <div class="num"><span class="range">3,000–4,000</span></div>
        <div class="src">
          <strong>Student leaders &amp; Chinese student unions abroad</strong>
          Figures circulated by protest leaders in the aftermath.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="toll">
        <div class="num"><span class="range">10,000+</span></div>
        <div class="src">
          <strong>UK ambassador Alan Donald</strong>
          Declassified diplomatic cable dated June 5, 1989, released by the UK National Archives in 2017.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="toll">
        <div class="num"><span class="range">10,000+</span></div>
        <div class="src">
          <strong>US Embassy (Beijing)</strong>
          Declassified US State Department cable from the same week, citing sources within the Chinese government.
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>

    <p class="note">
      <strong>On the numbers:</strong> The Tiananmen Mothers, a group of relatives of victims led by Ding Zilin,
      have documented 202 named victims by name and date of death as of the most recent published updates — a partial
      accounting that nonetheless contradicts any claim of a low toll. Discussion of the June 4th events remains
      heavily censored within mainland China, and no public memorial is permitted. The range of credible estimates
      spans two orders of magnitude, a gap that itself reflects the suppression of inquiry into the event.
    </p>
  </section>

</main>

<footer>
  <span>Chronology compiled from open historical sources</span>
  <span>Beijing · April – June 1989</span>
  <a href="#top">Return to top ↑</a>
</footer>

<script>
  // Reveal-on-scroll for timeline entries
  const entries = document.querySelectorAll('.entry');
  if ('IntersectionObserver' in window && !window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches){
    const io = new IntersectionObserver((hits) => {
      hits.forEach(h => { if (h.isIntersecting){ h.target.classList.add('in'); io.unobserve(h.target); } });
    }, { threshold: 0.15, rootMargin: '0px 0px -60px 0px' });
    entries.forEach(e => io.observe(e));
  } else {
    entries.forEach(e => e.classList.add('in'));
  }
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

A few notes on the choices behind the page:

- **Typographic tone.** The page uses a serif (Iowan/Palatino/Georgia stack) on a warm cream paper background with a subtle SVG grain layer, deliberately evoking newspaper or archival print — appropriate for a historical chronicle rather than a slick product page.
- **Color.** A deep oxblood red (`#8a1f1f`) is reserved exclusively for date kickers, critical-event markers on the timeline, and the casualty cards. It functions as a small, grave accent rather than decoration.
- **Timeline.** A single vertical rule with circular markers; entries flagged as "critical" (the April 26 editorial, the hunger strike, martial law, June 3, June 4) get filled red markers to give the chronology a visual rhythm and emphasize the escalation points.
- **Death toll section.** Rather than a single number, it presents six sources side by side — from the official 200–300 to the 10,000+ figures in declassified UK and US cables — with provenance for each. This is the honest treatment of a contested historical record where no consensus figure exists.
- **Motion.** A single restrained reveal-on-scroll using IntersectionObserver, disabled under `prefers-reduced-motion`. No auto-playing effects anywhere.
- **Sources.** The note credits the Tiananmen Mothers group's documented victim count, which is the most rigorously evidenced partial figure available.

Double-click the saved file to open it in any modern browser; everything is self-contained.

