</head>
<body>

<header>
  <div class="hero-eyebrow">Beijing · Spring 1989</div>
  <h1>The Tiananmen Square Massacre</h1>
  <p class="hero-sub">
    For seven weeks in the spring of 1989, students and citizens filled the heart of Beijing
    calling for democracy, free expression, and an end to corruption. On the night of
    June 3–4, the Chinese government sent in troops and tanks. This is a timeline of those events.
  </p>
  <div class="hero-dates">April 15 — June 5, 1989</div>
</header>

<!-- ======= Death toll ======= -->
<section class="toll">
  <div class="toll-card">
    <h2>The Death Toll</h2>
    <div class="toll-figures">
      <div class="figure">
        <div class="num">~200–300</div>
        <div class="label">Official Chinese government claim (including soldiers)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <div class="num">2,600</div>
        <div class="label">Chinese Red Cross initial report — later retracted under pressure</div>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <div class="num">Hundreds–1,000+</div>
        <div class="label">Most independent estimates of civilian deaths</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      The true number killed remains unknown. The Chinese government has never permitted an
      independent investigation and continues to censor discussion of the massacre. A British
      diplomatic cable declassified in 2017 suggested the toll could be far higher — a figure
      historians treat with caution. Many victims were ordinary workers and residents shot on
      the avenues approaching the square, especially at Muxidi in western Beijing.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ======= Timeline ======= -->
<div class="timeline-wrap">

  <h2 class="month-heading"><small>Part One</small>April</h2>
  <div class="timeline">
    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 15</div>
      <h3>The Death of Hu Yaobang</h3>
      <p>Hu Yaobang, a reform-minded Communist Party leader purged in 1987 for being too sympathetic to liberalization, dies of a heart attack. Students gather spontaneously in Tiananmen Square to mourn him — and mourning quickly becomes a call for political reform, freedom of the press, and action against corruption.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 17–18</div>
      <h3>Students March and Issue Demands</h3>
      <p>Thousands of university students march to the square, presenting demands including dialogue with the government, press freedom, disclosure of officials' assets, and democratic reforms.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 21–22</div>
      <h3>The Official Memorial</h3>
      <p>More than 100,000 people occupy the square on the eve of Hu's official memorial service. Three students kneel on the steps of the Great Hall of the People for hours, holding a petition aloft, but no official comes out to receive it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 26</div>
      <h3>The "Turmoil" Editorial</h3>
      <p>Reflecting Deng Xiaoping's views, a <em>People's Daily</em> editorial brands the movement a grave instance of "turmoil" threatening stability. Rather than intimidating students, the accusation of being unpatriotic galvanizes them.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="event major">
      <div class="date">April 27</div>
      <h3>The Great March</h3>
      <p>In defiant response to the editorial, tens of thousands of students — supported by cheering residents — break through police cordons and march for hours through Beijing. It is the largest unauthorized demonstration in the history of the People's Republic.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="month-heading"><small>Part Two</small>May</h2>
  <div class="timeline">
    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 4</div>
      <h3>The May Fourth Anniversary</h3>
      <p>On the 70th anniversary of the patriotic May Fourth Movement, students march again, framing their cause in the long tradition of Chinese youth demanding national renewal. Journalists join, calling for press freedom.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="event major">
      <div class="date">May 13</div>
      <h3>The Hunger Strike Begins</h3>
      <p>Hundreds of students begin a hunger strike in the square, timed to coincide with the visit of Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev. The act of sacrifice electrifies the city; eventually over a thousand students join, and medics ferry the collapsing to hospitals.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 15–18</div>
      <h3>A Million in the Streets</h3>
      <p>Gorbachev's state visit — the first Sino-Soviet summit in 30 years — is upstaged; the welcome ceremony must be moved from the square. Sympathy protests spread to over 400 cities, and more than a million people march in Beijing. Workers, teachers, civil servants, even police join in.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 19</div>
      <h3>Zhao Ziyang's Farewell</h3>
      <p>At dawn, Party General Secretary Zhao Ziyang, who has argued for a conciliatory approach, makes a tearful visit to the square. Speaking through a megaphone, he tells the students: "We came too late." It is his last public appearance; he is purged within days and spends the rest of his life under house arrest. The hunger strike is called off that evening.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="event major">
      <div class="date">May 20</div>
      <h3>Martial Law Declared</h3>
      <p>The government declares martial law in parts of Beijing. Troops move toward the city — but residents pour into the streets, blocking convoys, pleading with soldiers, and feeding them. The advance stalls; troops withdraw to the outskirts days later.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 30</div>
      <h3>The Goddess of Democracy</h3>
      <p>Art students unveil a 10-meter plaster statue, the Goddess of Democracy, facing the portrait of Mao across the square. Within days it will be crushed beneath a tank.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="month-heading"><small>Part Three</small>June</h2>
  <div class="timeline">
    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">June 1–2</div>
      <h3>The Decision Is Made</h3>
      <p>Party elders and hardliners finalize the decision to clear the square by force. Unarmed troops in civilian clothes attempt to infiltrate the city and are turned back. In the square, intellectuals — including Liu Xiaobo, later a Nobel Peace laureate — begin their own hunger strike to show solidarity.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">June 3 · Evening</div>
      <h3>Troops Open Fire</h3>
      <p>Armored columns of the People's Liberation Army move into Beijing from multiple directions. Residents build barricades and try to block them. Around 10 p.m., near Muxidi in western Beijing, troops fire live ammunition into crowds of civilians. Fighting spreads along Chang'an Avenue as citizens are shot while trying to halt the advance.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="june4 event">
    <div class="date">June 3–4 · Overnight</div>
    <h3>The Massacre</h3>
    <p>Through the night, troops and tanks fight their way to the square, firing on protesters, bystanders, and apartment blocks. Most of the killing occurs not in the square itself but on the western approaches, where residents are gunned down in the streets. In the early hours of June 4, after last-ditch negotiations, the remaining students file out of the square around dawn. By morning, the army holds the city. Hospitals overflow with the dead and wounded; some are ordered not to treat or count the victims. The government's operation is code-named, and remembered abroad, simply by the date: June Fourth.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="timeline">
    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">June 5</div>
      <h3>Tank Man</h3>
      <p>On Chang'an Avenue, a lone man in a white shirt, carrying shopping bags, steps in front of a column of tanks. He climbs onto the lead tank and appears to speak with its crew before being pulled away by bystanders. Photographed by journalists from a hotel

