    font-size: 0.85rem; margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="container">

  <header>
    <h1>The Tiananmen Square Massacre</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A timeline of the 1989 pro-democracy protests and their violent suppression, April – June 1989</p>
    <div class="rule"></div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <div class="timeline">

      <div class="event">
        <h2><span class="date">April 15</span> — Death of Hu Yaobang</h2>
        <p>Hu Yaobang, a reform-minded former Communist Party General Secretary who had been forced to resign in 1987, dies of a heart attack. Students gather in Tiananmen Square in Beijing to mourn him, and the gatherings quickly evolve into broader calls for political reform, freedom of the press, and action against corruption.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event">
        <h2><span class="date">April 17–22</span> — Protests grow</h2>
        <p>Thousands of students from Beijing universities march to the square, drafting a list of "Seven Demands" including dialogue with the government and greater freedoms. On April 22, during Hu Yaobang's official memorial, over 100,000 people gather; student representatives kneel on the steps of the Great Hall of the People, waiting in vain to present their petition to Premier Li Peng.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event">
        <h2><span class="date">April 26</span> — The "Turmoil" Editorial</h2>
        <p>The <em>People's Daily</em> publishes a front-page editorial branding the movement a "conspiracy" and "turmoil" aimed at overthrowing the government. Rather than intimidating students, the editorial — reflecting Party elder Deng Xiaoping's hard line — galvanizes the movement.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event">
        <h2><span class="date">April 27</span> — Mass march</h2>
        <p>In defiance of the editorial, an estimated 150,000–200,000 students and supporters march through Beijing, cheered on by huge crowds of citizens. Protests spread to cities across China.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event">
        <h2><span class="date">May 13</span> — Hunger strike begins</h2>
        <p>Frustrated by the government's refusal to hold dialogue, several hundred students begin a hunger strike in Tiananmen Square. The strike wins enormous public sympathy, and daily crowds in the square swell toward one million people.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event">
        <h2><span class="date">May 15–16</span> — Gorbachev's visit</h2>
        <p>Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev arrives in Beijing for a historic Sino-Soviet summit. Welcoming ceremonies must be moved from the square, deeply embarrassing the leadership. Many protesters see Gorbachev's reforms as a model for China.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event">
        <h2><span class="date">May 19–20</span> — Zhao Ziyang's plea and martial law</h2>
        <p>In the early hours of May 19, reformist General Secretary Zhao Ziyang makes a tearful visit to the hunger strikers, saying "We came too late." It is his last public appearance; he is purged and spends the rest of his life under house arrest. Martial law is declared on May 20, but citizens block army convoys, and troops initially withdraw.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event">
        <h2><span class="date">May 30</span> — Goddess of Democracy</h2>
        <p>Students from the Central Academy of Fine Arts unveil a 10-meter plaster statue, the "Goddess of Democracy," facing the portrait of Mao Zedong across the square. It becomes a global symbol of the movement.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event">
        <h2><span class="date">June 3</span> — Troops open fire</h2>
        <p>On the evening of June 3, tens of thousands of troops backed by tanks and armored vehicles advance on Beijing from multiple directions. Ordered to reach the square by dawn, soldiers fire live ammunition at civilians who block their path. The heaviest killing occurs along Chang'an Avenue and around Muxidi, west of the square, where residents are shot in the streets and in their homes.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event">
        <h2><span class="date">June 4</span> — The square is cleared</h2>
        <p>By the early morning of June 4, the military secures the city center. The remaining students negotiate a withdrawal from the square itself. Throughout the day, troops continue to fire on crowds of angry residents. The "Goddess of Democracy" is toppled by tanks.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event">
        <h2><span class="date">Aftermath</span> — Arrests and silence</h2>
        <p>In the weeks that follow, thousands are arrested across China; some workers accused of attacking soldiers are executed. On June 5, an unidentified man — "Tank Man" — briefly halts a column of tanks in an image seen worldwide. Public discussion of the events remains heavily censored in China to this day.</p>
      </div>

    </div>

    <section class="toll">
      <h2>Estimated Death Toll</h2>
      <p>No verified death toll exists. The Chinese government has never released a full accounting, and estimates vary widely:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The Chinese government officially claimed roughly <strong>200–300 deaths</strong>, including soldiers.</li>
        <li>The Chinese Red Cross initially reported <strong>2,600 deaths</strong> before retracting the figure under pressure.</li>
        <li>Independent estimates generally range from <strong>several hundred to several thousand</strong> civilians killed.</li>
        <li>A British diplomatic cable declassified in 2017 cited a source claiming approximately <strong>10,000 deaths</strong>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Most of the dead were killed not inside the square but on the avenues of western Beijing, particularly around Muxidi, as troops fought their way toward the city center.</p>
    </section>

    <p class="note">Sources for estimates include contemporary press reporting, human rights organizations, and declassified diplomatic documents. The true number of victims remains unknown.</p>
  </main>

  <footer>
    In memory of those who died in Beijing, June 1989.
  </footer>

</div>
</body>
</html>
```

**Notes on the content:**

- **Timeline scope:** Covers the arc from Hu Yaobang's death (the spark) through the crackdown and immediate aftermath, with 11 dated entries.
- **Death toll:** Presented honestly as a range, since no definitive figure exists — from the government's claim of 200–300 up to the 10,000 figure in the declassified 2017 British cable.
- **Accuracy detail:** The page notes that most killings occurred on the approach roads (especially Muxidi) rather than inside the square itself, which reflects the historical record.
- **Design:** A simple vertical timeline with a muted, somber palette; fully self-contained and responsive — just save it as an `.html` file and open it in any browser.

