</head>
<body>
<div class="page">

  <header>
    <div class="kicker">Historical Record · Spring 1989</div>
    <h1>The Tiananmen Square Protests and Massacre</h1>
    <p class="sub">A timeline of events from April to June 1989, when pro-democracy demonstrations in Beijing ended in a military crackdown.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="intro">
    In the spring of 1989, students and citizens across China gathered to mourn the death of
    reformist Communist Party leader Hu Yaobang and to call for political reform, an end to
    corruption, and greater democracy. Over seven weeks, the gatherings in Beijing's Tiananmen
    Square grew into one of the largest protest movements in modern Chinese history — and ended
    when the government sent in the military. The events remain heavily censored in mainland China.
  </div>

  <div class="timeline">

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 15, 1989</div>
      <h2>Death of Hu Yaobang</h2>
      <p>Hu Yaobang, a former General Secretary of the Chinese Communist Party known for his
      reformist views, dies of a heart attack. Students begin gathering in Beijing to mourn him,
      viewing him as a symbol of political openness.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 17–18</div>
      <h2>First gatherings in Tiananmen Square</h2>
      <p>Thousands of students march to Tiananmen Square, chanting slogans against corruption and
      calling for democracy and freedom of the press. Mourning gatherings quickly turn into
      political demonstrations.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 22</div>
      <h2>Memorial service — students occupy the Square</h2>
      <p>On the day of Hu Yaobang's memorial service, an estimated 100,000–200,000 students
      gather in the square. Student representatives attempt to present a petition of demands to
      government officials but are refused a meeting.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 26</div>
      <h2>The "turmoil" editorial</h2>
      <p>A <em>People's Daily</em> editorial denounces the protests as "turmoil" orchestrated by a
      small group of agitators. Rather than calming the movement, the editorial inflames it —
      protests spread to dozens of cities.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 4</div>
      <h2>May Fourth anniversary marches</h2>
      <p>Marking the 70th anniversary of the 1919 May Fourth Movement, hundreds of thousands of
      students and citizens march in Beijing and other major cities, reviving the movement's
      legacy of patriotic, democratic protest.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 13</div>
      <h2>Hunger strike begins</h2>
      <p>Roughly 3,000 students begin a hunger strike in Tiananmen Square, demanding dialogue
      with the government. The strike galvanizes public support, with citizens bringing food
      and supplies. Party General Secretary Zhao Ziyang visits the students and pleads with them
      to end the strike.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 15–18</div>
      <h2>Gorbachev's state visit</h2>
      <p>Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev visits Beijing for a Sino-Soviet summit — the first
      between the two countries in 30 years. The protests overshadow the visit and embarrass
      the Chinese leadership on the world stage.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 19</div>
      <h2>Martial law declared</h2>
      <p>Premier Li Peng declares martial law. In a final public appearance, Zhao Ziyang
      tearfully urges students to leave the square. Zhao is subsequently removed from power.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 20 – June 2</div>
      <h2>Citizens block the troops</h2>
      <p>Martial law takes effect, but Beijing residents — workers, students, and ordinary
      citizens — block army convoys from entering the city center, fraternizing with soldiers
      and persuading many units to turn back. Demonstrations spread to an estimated 400 cities
      across China. On May 30, protesters erect the "Goddess of Democracy" statue in the square.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 23</div>
      <h2>The largest demonstration</h2>
      <p>An estimated one million people — perhaps more — march through Beijing in support of
      the students, the largest demonstration of the entire movement.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event critical">
      <div class="date">June 3</div>
      <h2>Troops move into Beijing</h2>
      <p>The government orders the square cleared. In the evening, armed troops and armored
      vehicles begin advancing on central Beijing. Clashes erupt between soldiers and
      residents as troops push toward the city center.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event critical">
      <div class="date">Night of June 3–4</div>
      <h2>The crackdown</h2>
      <p>In the early hours of June 4, troops open fire on crowds and students in and around
      Tiananmen Square, using live ammunition against demonstrators and bystanders. Tanks and
      soldiers clear the square by force. The full extent of the violence is still debated,
      but hundreds — and by many accounts thousands — are killed.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">June 4 – June 5</div>
      <h2>Aftermath and "Tank Man"</h2>
      <p>On the morning of June 5, a lone man — later dubbed "Tank Man" — famously stands in
      front of a column of tanks leaving the square, halting it briefly before being pulled
      away. His identity and fate remain unknown. A worldwide crackdown on participants and
      organizers follows.</p>
    </div>

  </div>

  <section class="toll">
    <h2>Estimated death toll</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Source</th><th>Estimated deaths</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Chinese government (official, June 19, 1989)</td>
          <td>~241, including soldiers and "rioters"</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Chinese Red Cross (initial figure, later retracted under pressure)</td>
          <td>~2,600</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Declassified Western intelligence estimates</td>
          <td>Several hundred (roughly 180–500)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Student groups and unofficial Chinese accounts</td>
          <td>Several thousand</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Academic consensus</td>
          <td>At least several hundred; possibly over 1,000 — exact figure unknown</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">The true death toll remains disputed. The Chinese government has never
    released a complete account, and discussion of the events is censored in mainland China.
    Most historians agree the number of civilians killed was far higher than the official
    figure, but no definitive count exists.</p>
  </section>

  <footer>
    This page is a factual summary based on reporting by international news organizations,
    declassified government documents, and academic research. The events of June 1989 are
    widely documented outside China but remain a censored topic within mainland China.
    Figures marked as estimates reflect the range of serious scholarly and journalistic
    assessments.
  </footer>

</div>

<script>
  // Gentle fade-in as timeline entries scroll into view
  const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
    entries.forEach((entry) => {
      if (entry.isIntersecting) {
        entry.target.classList.add('visible');
        observer.unobserve(entry.target);
      }
    });
  }, { threshold: 0.15 });

  document.querySelectorAll('.event').forEach((el) => observer.observe(el));
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

