    <h1>The 1989 Tiananmen Square Protests</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A timeline of events, April – June 1989</p>
  </header>

  <div class="context">
    <strong>Context:</strong> Following the death of reformist Communist Party leader
    Hu Yaobang on April 15, 1989, students and citizens gathered in Beijing's
    Tiananmen Square to mourn him and call for political reform. Over seven weeks,
    the demonstrations grew into a mass movement before being forcibly cleared by
    the People's Liberation Army on the night of June 3–4, 1989.
  </div>

  <div class="timeline">

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">April 15, 1989</span>
      <h2>Death of Hu Yaobang</h2>
      <p>Hu Yaobang, a former General Secretary of the Communist Party known for his
      reformist views, dies of a heart attack. Students begin gathering in Tiananmen
      Square to mourn him.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">April 18 – 22</span>
      <h2>Mourning turns to protest</h2>
      <p>Tens of thousands of students march on Tiananmen Square and the Xinhua Gate
      of Zhongnanhai, calling for dialogue with the government and protesting
      corruption and lack of political reform.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">April 22</span>
      <h2>Hu Yaobang's memorial service</h2>
      <p>On the day of Hu's state memorial service, roughly 100,000 students gather
      in Tiananmen Square. Student representatives present petitions to government
      officials, who decline to meet them formally.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">April 26</span>
      <h2>The People's Daily editorial</h2>
      <p>A <em>People's Daily</em> editorial denounces the protests as "turmoil"
      orchestrated by a "small number of people." Rather than intimidating
      demonstrators, the editorial galvanizes larger protests across the country.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 4</span>
      <h2>May Fourth anniversary demonstrations</h2>
      <p>Marking the 70th anniversary of the 1919 May Fourth Movement, hundreds of
      thousands of students and citizens demonstrate in Beijing and other cities.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 13</span>
      <h2>Hunger strike begins</h2>
      <p>Several thousand students begin a hunger strike in Tiananmen Square,
      demanding formal dialogue with the government. Crowds in the square swell to
      over a million people.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 15</span>
      <h2>Gorbachev's visit</h2>
      <p>Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev visits Beijing for a Sino-Soviet summit —
      the first in 30 years. The massive student presence overshadows the
      diplomatic event and embarrasses the Chinese leadership.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 19</span>
      <h2>Martial law declared</h2>
      <p>Party General Secretary Zhao Ziyang makes a tearful televised appeal asking
      students to end the hunger strike. Premier Li Peng announces martial law,
      effective May 20.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 20 – June 2</span>
      <h2>Citizens block the troops</h2>
      <p>Hundreds of thousands of Beijing residents surround military vehicles and
      block troop convoys from reaching the city center, fraternizing with
      soldiers in some instances. The government's authority in the capital is
      effectively paralyzed.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 23</span>
      <h2>Million-person march</h2>
      <p>An estimated one million people march through Beijing in support of the
      students — one of the largest demonstrations in the city's history.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event critical">
      <span class="date">June 3 – 4, night</span>
      <h2>The crackdown</h2>
      <p>In the evening of June 3, troops with live ammunition advance on Beijing.
      Throughout the night and into the early hours of June 4, soldiers fire on
      crowds and students in and around Tiananmen Square. Armored vehicles and
      tanks push into the city center. By dawn, the square has been cleared.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event critical">
      <span class="date">June 4 – 5</span>
      <h2>Aftermath in the square</h2>
      <p>On the morning of June 4, a column of tanks exiting the square is blocked
      by a single unidentified man — the image known as "Tank Man" (June 5).
      Sporadic clashes continue in Beijing, and a military lockdown follows.
      Zhao Ziyang is removed from power; the protests are officially declared a
      "counter-revolutionary rebellion."</p>
    </div>

